<Flow xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!-- THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT INSIDE THE ROOT ELEMENT. A comment placed ABOVE <Flow>
         breaks `sf` deploy at source conversion with the misleading "unable to find matching
         parent xml file" error.

         =====================================================================
         2026-08-10 (code review 3B/3C, C4) - ENTRY CONDITION ADDED. NOTHING ELSE CHANGED.
         =====================================================================
         Until this change the <start> element carried NO filters and NO filterLogic, so this
         flow fired on EVERY Counter_Offer__c insert. 3B gave LOI__c a Disposition_LOI record
         type and a sell-side counter loop that reuses Counter_Offer__c, CounterOfferService and
         lwc/loiCounterOffer wholesale - which means THE FIRST DISPOSITION COUNTER WOULD HAVE
         NOTIFIED THE Acquisitions_Team PUBLIC GROUP about a sale. Recorded as a live leak at
         D15 finding #4 and as review critical C4; post-deploy gate T-D3 tests exactly this.

         🔴 THIS SUPPRESSES AN EXISTING NOTIFICATION. IT DOES NOT CREATE ONE. Read that before
         "restoring" the ungated start element. D9 puts every notification in this programme -
         all 14 on the disposition side - OUT OF SCOPE, and D15 finding #4 states the
         distinction explicitly: "Gating that flow is scope CONTAINMENT, not a new notification,
         and is therefore not covered by D9's deferral." Removing this criterion does not
         restore parity; it re-opens a cross-module leak.

         =====================================================================
         WHY A FORMULA AND NOT A CONDITION ROW.
         =====================================================================
         The discriminator is on the PARENT. Counter_Offer__c has exactly eight fields
         (Counter_Cap_Rate__c, Counter_Date__c, Counter_Price__c, Counter_Response__c,
         Direction__c, LOI__c, Revision_Number__c, Subsequent_Version__c) and carries NO
         Disposition__c lookup and NO record types - the same two absences that forced D21 to
         accept org-wide viewAllRecords on this object instead of the narrow D17 sharing shape.
         So there is nothing on the triggering record itself to filter on, and the criterion has
         to reach one hop up to LOI__r.

         A record-triggered flow's CONDITION BUILDER offers only fields on the triggering object;
         reaching a related record is what FORMULA entry conditions exist for (Winter '23, "Use
         Formulas as Flow Entry Conditions"), and a formula's $Record supports relationship
         traversal. Hence <filterFormula> rather than <filters>/<filterLogic> - the two are
         alternatives, never both.

         ⚠ ONE HOP ONLY, AND DELIBERATELY NOT A RECORD-TYPE TEST. $Record.LOI__r.Disposition__c
         is a single lookup traversal to a stored field. A $RecordType-style traversal is NOT
         available here, which is why the criterion keys on the LOOKUP rather than on
         LOI__r.RecordType.DeveloperName. That also makes it correct DURING the migration window:
         every LOI in the org still sits on the Master record type until gate T-A1/T-A2 run, so a
         record-type test would let every disposition counter through in exactly the window this
         gate matters most. The lookup is populated at creation by CounterOfferService's parent,
         so it is authoritative from row one. This is the same precedence order
         CounterOfferService.isSaleSide uses in the opposite direction (record type first, lookup
         as the fallback) - it can afford record-type-first because it also has the lookup; this
         flow has only the lookup.

         ⚠ NO IN-REPO PRECEDENT FOR <filterFormula> - this is the first one. Every other flow
         here uses <filters>. Confirm at check-only that the element is accepted at API 67.0, and
         confirm in the org that an ACQUISITION counter still produces the notification (an entry
         condition that silently matches nothing looks identical to a working gate from outside).

         =====================================================================
         fTitle NOW TREATS 'Buyer' AS THE COUNTERPARTY. THE EARLIER "LEFT ALONE" NOTE WAS WRONG.
         =====================================================================
         Review C4 noted the second order defect: fTitle branched on Direction__c = 'Seller' only,
         and 3B made the counterparty token on a sale 'Buyer', so a BUYER's counter fell to the else
         branch and announced "LOI counter sent to seller" when the buyer sent it.

         🔴 THAT BRANCH WAS ORIGINALLY LEFT ALONE ON THE GROUND THAT THE GATE ABOVE REMOVED THE
         ENTIRE POPULATION THAT COULD REACH IT. THE CLAIM IS NOT TRUE AND IS RETRACTED HERE.
         CounterOfferService.isSaleSide is RECORD TYPE FIRST and falls back to the lookup, so an
         LOI__c carrying the Disposition_LOI record type with a BLANK Disposition__c lookup is sale
         side to the SERVICE (it derives the counterparty as 'Buyer' and stores it) and acquisition
         side to THIS GATE (ISBLANK is true, so the flow fires). LOI__c.Disposition__c is
         <required>false</required>, so that state is reachable through the ordinary UI. The
         identical defect and the identical correction were found one object over on
         flows/PSA_Version_Notify; the two fTitle formulas now read the same.

         The widening is a STRICT NO OP on every value the acquisition side actually produces:
         CounterOfferService stores 'Ours' or 'Seller' on an acquisition parent, and both take
         exactly the branch they took before. Only 'Buyer', which was wrong wherever it appeared,
         changes. Both 'Seller' and 'Buyer' mean THE COUNTERPARTY per the Direction__c token
         contract; 'Ours' means DPEG on either side.

         🔴 RESIDUAL, NAMED RATHER THAN FIXED: for that same blank lookup row the GATE also fails
         open, so Acquisitions_Team still hears about a sale, merely with a correct title now. This
         file cannot close that. The formula's only discriminator IS the blank lookup, and the one
         that would separate the two populations, LOI__r.RecordType.DeveloperName, is a SECOND hop,
         which an entry formula does not support (see the ONE HOP note above). The real fix is
         upstream: make Disposition__c required on the Disposition_LOI record type, or have the
         service refuse a sale side write without it. Both are outside this one file edit. The else
         branch's "sent to seller" is acquisition vocabulary and would name DPEG on a sale; it is
         left because on a purchase DPEG IS the buyer, so it is literally right for 'Ours'.
    -->
    <actionCalls>
        <name>Notify_Counter</name>
        <label>Notify Counter</label>
        <locationX>176</locationX>
        <locationY>288</locationY>
        <actionName>GroupNotifier</actionName>
        <actionType>apex</actionType>
        <flowTransactionModel>CurrentTransaction</flowTransactionModel>
        <inputParameters>
            <name>recipientGroup</name>
            <value>
                <stringValue>Acquisitions_Team</stringValue>
            </value>
        </inputParameters>
        <inputParameters>
            <name>title</name>
            <value>
                <elementReference>fTitle</elementReference>
            </value>
        </inputParameters>
        <inputParameters>
            <name>body</name>
            <value>
                <elementReference>fBody</elementReference>
            </value>
        </inputParameters>
        <inputParameters>
            <name>targetRecordId</name>
            <value>
                <elementReference>$Record.LOI__c</elementReference>
            </value>
        </inputParameters>
        <nameSegment>GroupNotifier</nameSegment>
    </actionCalls>
    <apiVersion>67.0</apiVersion>
    <description>Notifies Acquisitions_Team when a counter offer is logged. Gated 2026-08-10 to ACQUISITION LOIs only (LOI__r.Disposition__c blank). That criterion SUPPRESSES an over-broad existing notification: scope containment, not a new one. Do not remove it.</description>
    <environments>Default</environments>
    <formulas>
        <name>fBody</name>
        <dataType>String</dataType>
        <expression>&apos;Counter price: &apos; &amp; IF(ISBLANK({!$Record.Counter_Price__c}), &apos;n/a&apos;, TEXT({!$Record.Counter_Price__c}))</expression>
    </formulas>
    <formulas>
        <name>fTitle</name>
        <dataType>String</dataType>
        <expression>IF(OR(ISPICKVAL({!$Record.Direction__c}, &apos;Seller&apos;), ISPICKVAL({!$Record.Direction__c}, &apos;Buyer&apos;)),
&apos;LOI counter received - ball in our court&apos;,
&apos;LOI counter sent to seller&apos;)</expression>
    </formulas>
    <interviewLabel>Counter Offer Notify {!$Flow.CurrentDateTime}</interviewLabel>
    <label>Counter Offer Notify</label>
    <processType>AutoLaunchedFlow</processType>
    <start>
        <locationX>50</locationX>
        <locationY>0</locationY>
        <connector>
            <targetReference>Notify_Counter</targetReference>
        </connector>
        <filterFormula>ISBLANK({!$Record.LOI__r.Disposition__c})</filterFormula>
        <object>Counter_Offer__c</object>
        <recordTriggerType>Create</recordTriggerType>
        <triggerType>RecordAfterSave</triggerType>
    </start>
    <status>Active</status>
</Flow>
